#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228930 :question/id #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21765" :question/problem "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{78}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ab1d837-f782-5439-8e25-3c764f9cb5c5" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672803 :question-generator/id #uuid "19bcbbe2-d88a-5e62-a8ad-f6293745da71" :question-generator/script "generators/93/933a4c5b77f0df65680e8f6f53ef87afe3699ad4546a8e42bf853e236d82144b/17592186229182.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db66dec-2792-5218-8a9d-85b9dbca389e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228334} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4e072-1f4c-5373-a3d6-0795c277ede4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228335} {:answer/id #uuid "fd684dc0-61aa-5e73-9b90-b9380e77f537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228336} {:answer/id #uuid "37b72754-baa1-5d83-b734-0964e3861b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228337} {:answer/id #uuid "39eb6365-d2f3-5e96-a701-c71bfda37768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37b72754-baa1-5d83-b734-0964e3861b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228337} :answer-field/id #uuid "579dbc44-8b7c-5a48-b291-b80099c940f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228333}] :question/id #uuid "5708b503-7d42-5d87-80c5-062229fe4d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336439" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=0$ into the stated model $Q(t)=\\alpha+\\beta e^{-kt}$. Since $e^0=1$ and the initial salt mass is $6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $\\alpha+\\beta=Q(0)=6$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501500} {:answer/id #uuid "14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501506} {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} {:answer/id #uuid "df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501913} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.99\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad053d7-10e4-578d-b606-6bda94abf500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310f29d9-e5f9-5829-9c84-72d1324e1777" :question/math-academy-id "q-336738" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+10}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3f26d4-7fd0-5dfc-b30a-66eaf31e8b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377251} {:answer/id #uuid "59e1559d-1a25-5f5b-86a0-b44ad480a144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377252} {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} {:answer/id #uuid "3dca446e-f8bb-5a17-9561-bc2230c8306c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.42\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377254} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ad44-b315-5215-8406-035745dd6ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} :answer-field/id #uuid "753683c1-dfc0-5785-8a4b-d335ca58b088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01365019-dd55-56aa-aa06-e538a69722fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336743" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved preservative.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative is pumped into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of preservative in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which preservative enters the tank minus the rate at which preservative leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which preservative enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *preservative* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which preservative exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which preservative leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $18\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+18t-12t=(420+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of preservative in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *preservative* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(70+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =0.9(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =0.9(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.9(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of preservative. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 0.3(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 21+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-11 \\\\ C & =-11(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)-\\frac{11(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.3(70+t)-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =0.3(70+15)-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =25.5-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{85}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈18.04\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} {:answer/id #uuid "d27a819d-3733-5724-9e0a-425c1701164a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac1a8be-c213-5433-be78-80bb938c8e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377297} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1b189-1202-5081-99fc-577118ac8811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377298} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a95ae-28ad-5afb-9758-ae8c6c582bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c0a0d5-4bf3-54b2-a43d-067aa74d6301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377294}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "456aacfd-49e4-5a1e-8363-4f5725f307ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-339394" :question/problem "A juice-processing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a liquid solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+20t-30t=(400-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(40-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(40-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q) & =(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (40-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(40-t)^{-4}Q & =0.6(40-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3}) & =0.6(40-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(40-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40-t)^{-3} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(40-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)+C(40-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ C(40-0)^{3}+0.3(40-0) & =4 \\\\ C(40)^{3}+12 & =4 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}(40-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 40\\,\\left(\\frac{40-t}{40}\\right)-8{\\left(\\frac{40-t}{40}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})-8{(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-8,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-8)=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f681e08e-700d-5f11-a546-1b73d4a5d646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377302} {:answer/id #uuid "b28e0e34-3fc5-53eb-9f95-3e5de6d384b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377303} {:answer/id #uuid "4b12db81-cdb5-5c78-aff0-867122be9ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377304} {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} {:answer/id #uuid "87a222e1-6c03-558b-9d4f-e7aa60b98bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c722bf6-15d0-5bfc-a832-c8c844cbaa11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377301}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35a9dbe2-5b3d-52d0-b235-b8068a9e0f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339396" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sweetener enters the tank minus the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sweetener enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sweetener* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $24\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+24t-32t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sweetener in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sweetener* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(32\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(32\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q=1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-4}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ (30-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(30-t)^{-5}Q & =1.2(30-t)^{-4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4}) & =1.2(30-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4})\\,dt & =\\int 1.2(30-t)^{-4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-4} & =1.2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30-t)^{-3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)+C(30-t)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of sweetener. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ C(30-0)^{4}+0.4(30-0) & =7 \\\\ C(30)^{4}+12 & =7 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}(30-t)^{4} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)-5{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})-5{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-5,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-5)=17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7203da68-a7dc-5fa7-a821-14e51c37d796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377309} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b57f-b0fe-5ca4-bc6e-be09e8b780b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.97\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377311} {:answer/id #uuid "57a068be-87ec-53cf-a457-ecd6c727cbfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.92\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377312} {:answer/id #uuid "08fda585-15a3-51ba-b2f7-6c5b1fb1b63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ba31-9d56-5bad-87cb-702df2d10932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6bee6b8-8870-5e26-916b-bf3b0601ff8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339399" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor is pumped into the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the tank minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+16t-12t=(400+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(100+t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(100+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)^{3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (100+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100+t)^{2}Q & =0.8(100+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3}) & =0.8(100+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(100+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t)^{3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(100+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)+\\frac{C}{(100+t)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ 0.2(100+0)+\\frac{C}{(100+0)^{3}} & =6 \\\\ 20+\\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =-14 \\\\ C & =-14(100)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)-\\frac{14(100)^{3}}{(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =0.2(100+t)-14{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+t}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =0.2(100+12)-14{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+12}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =22.4-14{\\left(\\frac{25}{28}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈12.44\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099acc92-7fac-5696-b31e-f1fb2df34af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.58\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377316} {:answer/id #uuid "b84c947f-ccfb-56fc-b617-b376d1a795a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377317} {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} {:answer/id #uuid "d84483d6-8474-5c8e-8b9c-2b641cc22149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.67\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377319} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77d91-31cc-548b-acc6-bdc54133b6cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d825b7-f752-5a4c-9bbe-c55b7b40186e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377315}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46db6f1f-9edd-53d3-9387-f26ef7512b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-339400" :question/problem "A fermentation vat initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient is pumped into the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $10\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+10t-5t=(500+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the vat, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & =100+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=100+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (100+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.6(100+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)) & =0.6(100+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(100+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t) & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(100+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)+\\frac{C}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ 0.3(100+0)+\\frac{C}{100+0} & =8 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{100} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{100} & =-22 \\\\ C & =-2200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)-\\frac{2200}{100+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.3(100+8)-\\frac{2200}{100+8} \\\\  & =32.4-\\frac{2200}{108} \\\\  & ≈12.03\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa46099-da71-5b6e-bf88-ae9c1cd9cdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501493} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8aabfa-3b2a-546d-a525-0b6c5247d515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501570} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64925c-c3fb-50bc-a4cf-26896798c5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501573} {:answer/id #uuid "79afc2f1-79a1-5645-98b6-d23030be32d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501693} {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5345499-fd39-5582-a566-851fc68ed4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501930} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f525641-4d00-5cf1-b519-6a6c8d3e8e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336746" :question/problem "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)+3{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19631a4f-fc75-5f16-94d0-c840626309aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.48\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501515} {:answer/id #uuid "409f3cff-59d6-5382-900e-2722da60aa57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.09\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501584} {:answer/id #uuid "5688b277-ef03-5eaf-8c63-160fb654c7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.62\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501625} {:answer/id #uuid "5f94b143-c3c7-545e-847d-30ec918b7ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.90\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501641} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b4fa8a-26bd-5a01-bcb0-d0ebf5922738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501768} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c957ef-ec71-5945-b112-c39defc2facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502016}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0340e70-b947-5b25-8e24-a63ff0f87f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-339403" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment basin initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved neutralizing agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent is pumped into the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093fa62e-8096-5d11-a95f-85739516d8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540395} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6c709b-6491-5318-be01-b2892d94e1a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540419} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0091a3-591b-5df5-a23c-7e2504de52d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540420} {:answer/id #uuid "97de21b0-ff26-5a3c-8296-017bf39aad56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540423} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3a8fe1-cd84-5092-a7c0-1ca7d988d36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc3a8fe1-cd84-5092-a7c0-1ca7d988d36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540436} :answer-field/id #uuid "75267109-2433-5e86-a308-d103295ada57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "020bf2b5-6b7c-5949-b44d-18f12c8b1668" :question/problem "A juice-processing tank initially holds $1200\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $22\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $36\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{100})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{100})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dissolved substance in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. The amount entering minus the amount leaving gives\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}.$$\nThe units of these rates are $\\text{kg/min}.$\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which substance enters the tank.\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{in}}&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\\\\n&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\\\&=0.96\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which substance exits the tank.\nThe volume is\n$$V(t)=1200+24t-36t=(1200-12t)\\,\\text{L}.$$\nThe concentration is $\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.$ Hence,\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{out}}&=(36\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\\\\n&=(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\\\\n&=\\frac{36Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/min}=\\frac{3Q}{100-t}\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}&=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}\\\\&=0.96-\\frac{3Q}{100-t}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThus,\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100-t}Q=0.96.$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n$$\\begin{aligned}I(t)&=e^{\\int 3/(100-t)\\,dt}\\\\&=e^{-3\\ln|100-t|}\\\\&=e^{\\ln (100-t)^{-3}}\\\\&=(100-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nMultiplying both sides by $I(t)=(100-t)^{-3}$ yields\n$$\\begin{aligned}(100-t)^{-3}\\cdot(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100-t}Q)&=(100-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.96\\\\\n(100-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100-t)^{-4}Q&=0.96(100-t)^{-3}\\\\\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-3})&=0.96(100-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-3})\\,dt&=\\int 0.96(100-t)^{-3}\\,dt\\\\Q(100-t)^{-3}&=0.96\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}(100-t)^{-2}+C\\\\Q(t)&=0.48(100-t)+C(100-t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\nThe initial condition is $Q(0)=22.$ Thus,\n$$\\begin{aligned}0.48(100-0)+C(100-0)^{3}&=22\\\\\n48+C(100)^{3}&=22\\\\C(100)^{3}&=-26\\\\C&=- \\frac{13}{500000}.\\end{aligned}$$\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n$$Q(t)=- \\frac{12 t}{25} - \\frac{13 \\left(100 - t\\right)^{3}}{500000} + 48.$$\nWriting this in the supplied normalized form gives\n$$Q(t)=48(1-\\frac{t}{100})-26(1-\\frac{t}{100})^{3}.$$\nFinally, we have $\\alpha=48,\\beta=-26,$ and\n$$\\alpha-\\beta=48-(-26)=74.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166fed0c-8918-56b2-b636-cf592f997ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540396} {:answer/id #uuid "5a22b298-1a5a-5058-9b77-2eab6913c677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540407} {:answer/id #uuid "69d45437-7fd6-5848-923b-1f4b15b57c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540412} {:answer/id #uuid "89c99d91-82cb-5e14-8527-fde394dd6d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540418} {:answer/id #uuid "a271f298-37bd-5138-badb-b61de5604cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d45437-7fd6-5848-923b-1f4b15b57c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540412} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d60cb95-ef2a-5a65-ad01-ba8fd07daf1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540402}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17255c70-5c1b-504e-b42a-7ed81673de48" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $1200L$ of water containing $18kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/100)+\\beta(1-0/100)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $18\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=18.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e4ada2a-5139-5032-b4de-d205305460f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540409} {:answer/id #uuid "6007bd78-951f-5937-8b7f-594e98a3abc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540411} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7bba5f-763e-535c-8b1c-41f26255be3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540414} {:answer/id #uuid "a051d0eb-a8b6-5a4f-a659-75e7cc394c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540426} {:answer/id #uuid "f4aec868-1b73-5283-b31d-1022e36884f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4ada2a-5139-5032-b4de-d205305460f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540409} :answer-field/id #uuid "828c2283-2e5c-57b1-9341-508812e68604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e92f0de-083d-594d-8418-04d4a2f47fe9" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $1200L$ of water containing $25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/100)+\\beta(1-0/100)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $25\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ee8d36-4c42-55e1-b036-f809d38523a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540393} {:answer/id #uuid "979b83a8-08ce-51ef-9610-41cb9724c059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540422} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d874e7-ecc5-5ceb-8ab5-01eb82e2b178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540429} {:answer/id #uuid "b56f7750-2da0-5f0d-ac6e-d4f8f62bcd16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540433} {:answer/id #uuid "c358f27c-4a0b-5503-9c72-b91feb37b6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56f7750-2da0-5f0d-ac6e-d4f8f62bcd16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540433} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5101218-0fea-5d27-a833-36a27156c7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99565b3b-f61a-552f-ba68-fcc345a63654" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $700L$ of water containing $20kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/70)+\\beta(1-0/70)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $20\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=20.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02508b23-4879-5a06-8cd4-c201a60347be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540392} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd9f448-5bde-502d-b6fa-d6ecc4208260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540399} {:answer/id #uuid "41bdeed0-e77e-5231-b995-54f921af41c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540405} {:answer/id #uuid "91a029ba-72e4-5a6f-9823-60d5a4002081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540421} {:answer/id #uuid "af605959-7d37-5e5f-b508-81c356e6b718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41bdeed0-e77e-5231-b995-54f921af41c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540405} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2ebc1c-a830-5456-871e-18cca410e54b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5adb393-bda2-5a89-ba8