on/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adf5ebb4-fcd4-57de-935c-1c7aa0634fba" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $13kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.05kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/50)+\\beta(1-0/50)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $13\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=13.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9cb43b-d28f-5ebe-81f2-e5f06d53503e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540400} {:answer/id #uuid "5de82b49-8912-5fcf-ab8d-93e04cb8d4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540408} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5ebcbc-8c69-5cc9-bf5c-823ff2e14600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540410} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d2b86-3505-5e3f-8791-342345d726c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540413} {:answer/id #uuid "aa91f25f-da97-5d4c-83a0-9983d280ff08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa91f25f-da97-5d4c-83a0-9983d280ff08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42e40e6-0759-5913-8d45-3a2bc6a1081c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c92cf723-bd03-5192-8480-80c9e6d48364" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $1200\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $36\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $48\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{100})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{100})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dissolved substance in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. The amount entering minus the amount leaving gives\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}.$$\nThe units of these rates are $\\text{kg/min}.$\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which substance enters the tank.\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{in}}&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot(36\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\\\\n&=(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\\\&=1.44\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which substance exits the tank.\nThe volume is\n$$V(t)=1200+36t-48t=(1200-12t)\\,\\text{L}.$$\nThe concentration is $\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.$ Hence,\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q'_{\\text{out}}&=(48\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\\\\n&=(48\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot(\\frac{Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\\\\n&=\\frac{48Q}{1200-12t}\\,\\text{kg/min}=\\frac{4Q}{100-t}\\,\\text{kg/min}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}&=Q'_{\\text{in}}-Q'_{\\text{out}}\\\\&=1.44-\\frac{4Q}{100-t}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThus,\n$$\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q=1.44.$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n$$\\begin{aligned}I(t)&=e^{\\int 4/(100-t)\\,dt}\\\\&=e^{-4\\ln|100-t|}\\\\&=e^{\\ln (100-t)^{-4}}\\\\&=(100-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nMultiplying both sides by $I(t)=(100-t)^{-4}$ yields\n$$\\begin{aligned}(100-t)^{-4}\\cdot(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q)&=(100-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.44\\\\\n(100-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(100-t)^{-5}Q&=1.44(100-t)^{-4}\\\\\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-4})&=1.44(100-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100-t)^{-4})\\,dt&=\\int 1.44(100-t)^{-4}\\,dt\\\\Q(100-t)^{-4}&=1.44\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}(100-t)^{-3}+C\\\\Q(t)&=0.48(100-t)+C(100-t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}$$\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\nThe initial condition is $Q(0)=10.$ Thus,\n$$\\begin{aligned}0.48(100-0)+C(100-0)^{4}&=10\\\\\n48+C(100)^{4}&=10\\\\C(100)^{4}&=-38\\\\C&=- \\frac{19}{50000000}.\\end{aligned}$$\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n$$Q(t)=- \\frac{12 t}{25} - \\frac{19 \\left(100 - t\\right)^{4}}{50000000} + 48.$$\nWriting this in the supplied normalized form gives\n$$Q(t)=48(1-\\frac{t}{100})-38(1-\\frac{t}{100})^{4}.$$\nFinally, we have $\\alpha=48,\\beta=-38,$ and\n$$\\alpha-\\beta=48-(-38)=86.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36064afd-4624-50aa-b9f7-869ddad593ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540613} {:answer/id #uuid "3e30535b-824e-5d84-96a9-2fa046e67302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540632} {:answer/id #uuid "576b7268-d013-5459-8484-febaf9a3d59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd73e85-3ada-52db-9939-f4f819c081ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} :answer-field/id #uuid "889a0978-0193-5e72-b84c-66f0c08de47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6915e10-1543-50b0-a358-2c56a1366444" :question/math-academy-id "q-336744" :question/problem "A fermentation tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of minerals enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *minerals* enter the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}(18-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 18\\,\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)-2.2{\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =7.2\\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})-2.2{(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =7.2,\\beta =-2.2,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =7.2-(-2.2)=9.4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1a4008-eb6b-5e44-90ed-d8dc9f2de41c" :answer/type {:db/ident